00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.519 align:start position:0%
Assassin's Creed has a turbulent past  the series reached its peak back in 2009  with Assassin's Creed 2 and has since  tried to recapture the magic sure there  have been some standout games in recent  years like Black Flag but there have  also been some flops like unity with  Assassin's Creed origins it's clear that  Ubisoft is taking big steps in order to  give the Assassin's Creed franchise the  shot in the arm it desperately needs now  if you're a games come this year you can  play the same demo I did but if not  don't forget to subscribe that way you  won't miss any of our games comm 2017  coverage Assassin's Creed origins is a  prologue to the entire series it takes  place in ancient Egypt while Cleopatra  is ascending the throne  you play as byuk a Magi warrior  intertwine in the politics of the era as  with every Assassin's Creed protagonist  byuk is fast quiet and deadly and you're  free to tackle any given situation as  you see fit I typically linked or my bow  for most encounters but there was a good  variety of both heavy and light melee  weapons one of origins biggest changes  is the combat I hate making this  comparison oh so very much but it plays  a lot like Dark Souls light rather than  chaining together a series of pairing  and countering animations like in  previous AC games you're in control of  every swing this means you need to know  the range and the speed of your weapon  otherwise your enemy will get the upper  hand and the best way to create an  opening in your enemies attacks are by  dodging and rolling learning attack  animations and knowing when to dodge is  key based on the demos I've played the  combat is far more engaging than in  previous Assassin's Creed games I felt  more like a player than a spectator as I  timed my attacks and dodges the changes  to combat also forced me to be more  aware of my surroundings who I was  fighting and what weapon I should use  unfortunately combat isn't as tight as  it could be the effectiveness of my  dodge role felt inconsistent at times  when I had time to my dodge perfectly  I'd still get hit fortunately origins  isn't nearly as demanding as a Souls  game so this shouldn't ignite any  fits of rage but it could be an issue in  fighting higher-level enemies however  the biggest draw for me is the setting  Assassin's Creed has always been about  time in place and origins is no  different  seeing Egypt during this ancient time  period is breathtaking at times the demo  area was limited to a small section of a  huge map but there is still a staggering  amount of detail seeing NPCs go about  their business in bustling marketplaces  makes the world feel alive and now that  Ubisoft has opted to remove the mini-map  in favor of an unobtrusive Skyrim like  compass I am far more engaged in the  world and my surroundings rather than  honing in on small symbols that pop up  on the map  unfortunately the occasional bug like a  chicken falling from the sky or a pesky  character walking through a cutscene can  break the immersion but hopefully the  kinks get worked out upon full release  otherwise we might end up with another  Assassin's Creed unity but my biggest  concern with origins is its story in  writing since Ezio the series hasn't had  a particularly strong protagonist and  byuk didn't make a great first  impression he's very serious and seems  to follow his credo rigorously he's not  all that interested in what's going on  around him either he just kind of did  things because a character told him to  my ex-wife on the other hand was a bit  more interesting she wasn't as reserved  as byuk and willing to break some rules  in order to get what she wants to  protect Egypt I would kill a thousand  priests  you and I are pledged to violence now  and always it could be interesting to  see how they play off each other over  the course of the game  assassin's creed origin seems like a  huge improvement over previous games but  if the writing falls flat I don't know  if it will leave behind the same legacy  that Assassin's Creed - and to a lesser  extent Black Flag did before it but  we'll find all that out on October 27th  when the game launches on ps4 Xbox and  PC if you have any specific questions  about my time with the game feel free to  reach out on Twitter at Jacob deck and  for more games comm coverage don't  forget to subscribe
 

